abel":"Scoliosis","hpo_id":"HP:0002650","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":true,"other_cause":false},{"text":"Umbilical and inguinal hernias","hpo_label":"Umbilical hernia","hpo_id":"HP:0001537","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":true,"other_cause":false},{"text":"Congenital talipes equinovarus","hpo_label":"Talipes equinovarus","hpo_id":"HP:0001762","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":true,"other_cause":false},{"text":"Pectus excavatum","hpo_label":"Pectus excavatum","hpo_id":"HP:0000767","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":true,"other_cause":false},{"text":"Gastroesophageal reflux","hpo_label":"Gastroesophageal reflux","hpo_id":"HP:0002020","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":true,"other_cause":false},{"text":"Intrauterine growth retardation and failure to thrive","hpo_label":"Intrauterine growth retardation","hpo_id":"HP:0001511","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":true,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"SNIP1","as_reported":"NM_024700.4:c.1097A>G, p.(Glu366Gly)","cdna":"c.1097A>G","protein":"p.Glu366Gly","genomic":"Chr1:g.37537842T>C","zygosity":"homozygous","origin":"inherited","consequence":"missense","key":"V1"}],"variant_roles":["causal"],"segregation":"Homozygous in all 35 affected; all parents heterozygous; unaffected sibs heterozygous or wild type","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"Puffenberger et al. 2012","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["35 of 51 identified individuals from 21 interrelated Old Order Amish families (19 males; 16 females, aged 1 month to 26 years) with molecularly confirmed SNIP1-related disorder were evaluated","confirmed homozygosity of the SNIP1 variant in 35 individuals, with all parents being heterozygous for the variant and all unaffected siblings being heterozygous for the variant or wild type","Neuroimaging was available for 18 individuals, all confirmed to have abnormalities"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"22279524","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"biallelic variants"}],"notes":[{"id":"N:34570759:1","pmid":"34570759","section":"mapping","statement":"Ammous et al. (2021) carried out homozygosity mapping in 4 affected Amish individuals from 2 nuclear families and found a single shared 1.65-Mb region of homozygosity on chromosome 1p34.3 (between rs6667450 and rs10889902) that contained 17 genes.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["identified a single region of homozygosity greater than 1Mb common to all four affected individuals, a small (1.65Mb) genomic region on chromosome 1p34.3 delimited by SNP markers rs6667450 and rs10889902"],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:34570759:2","pmid":"34570759","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Using whole-exome sequencing, Ammous et al. (2021) found that the only potentially pathogenic variant in the critical interval was the SNIP1 c.1097A>G (p.Glu366Gly) variant in exon 4. The variant segregated with an autosomal recessive condition. The affected residue is highly conserved and lies close to the C-terminal FHA domain.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["WES data on two individuals (X:16 and X:42) identified only a single potentially pathogenic sequence variant (Chr1:g.37537842T>C; RefSeq NM_024700.4:c.1097A>G, p.(Glu366Gly) [hg38]) in exon four of the SNIP1 gene","Multiple species alignment confirms this amino acid is highly conserved across species orthologs (Fig 1G) and is located close to the FHA domain at the SNIP1 C-terminus"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:34570759:3","pmid":"34570759","section":"population_genetics","statement":"Ammous et al. (2021) found the SNIP1 p.Glu366Gly allele at a frequency of 0.5% in Pennsylvania Amish and 1.4% in Ohio/Indiana/Wisconsin Amish (control database of more than 5,000 individuals), with no unaffected homozygotes. In gnomAD, its allele frequency was 0.001%, with 11 heterozygotes, 10 of whom were Amish.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["the SNIP1 NM_024700.4:c.1097A>G, p.(Glu366Gly) variant is present at an allele frequency of between 0.5% (Pennsylvania Amish) and 1.4% (Ohio/Indiana/Wisconsin Amish) in this founder population, with no unaffected individuals being homozygous for this variant","The variant is present in gnomAD (v3.1) with an allele frequency of 0.001% with 11 heterozygotes (10 Amish) and no homozygous individuals listed"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:34570759:4","pmid":"34570759","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Ammous et al. (2021) identified 51 Old Order Amish individuals with SNIP1-related disorder from 27 sibships, 12 of whom had died (between age 1 week and 11 years). Of these, 49 were confirmed homozygous for the variant, and 2 were not genotyped but had affected sibs who were homozygous.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["A total of fifty-one Old Order Amish individuals were identified with SNIP1-related disorder, among 27 sibships. Seventy-six percent (n = 39) are currently living (age range 6 months to 30 years), while 24% (n = 12) are deceased (age of death between 1 week and 11 years)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:34570759:5","pmid":"34570759","section":"gene_function","statement":"Ammous et al. (2021) performed blood transcriptome sequencing on 6 affected individuals and found 75 significantly upregulated and 109 significantly downregulated genes. The most overrepresented pathway was TGF-beta receptor signaling in epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition. Among the dysregulated genes were seizure-associated genes such as ROBO1, which was the most upregulated gene, and CNTNAP2.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["identified a list of 75 significantly upregulated genes, and 109 significantly downregulated genes","the most overrepresented pathway was the TGF-β receptor signalling in epithelial to mesenchyme pathway with differential expression of three genes (upregulation of PARD3 and downregulation of F11R and RPS27A)","This includes five genes (ROBO1, SOX5, CNTNAP2, PAFAH1B1 and TSNARE1) associated with seizures"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:34570759:6","pmid":"34570759","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Ammous et al. (2021) noted that Snip1-null mice die as embryos, which suggested that the Amish variant is unlikely to cause complete loss of function.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["SNIP1 null mouse models display embryonic lethality indicating that this Amish variant may be unlikely to result in complete loss of function"],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:34570759:7","pmid":"34570759","section":"clinical_management","statement":"Ammous et al. (2021) recommended elective gastrostomy tube placement and careful optimization of anticonvulsants to treat infantile apnea and prevent status epilepticus. They also recommended early EEG, neuroimaging and echocardiography at diagnosis, monitoring for hypoglycemia and hypothyroidism, and yearly ophthalmology and audiology assessments.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["We recommend elective gastrostomy tube placement to support growth and limit pulmonary aspiration, as well as careful optimisation of anticonvulsant medications to treat apnea in infancy, maintain seizure control, and prevent status epilepticus and consequent developmental regression.","Individuals should also be monitored for hypoglycemia and hypothyroidism as these are easily treatable. Yearly ophthalmology and audiology assessments are recommended."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:22279524:1","pmid":"22279524","section":"mapping","statement":"Puffenberger et al. (2012) used autozygosity mapping with 10K SNP arrays to map symptomatic epilepsy and skull dysplasia in Old Order Amish patients to a 6.6-Mb region on chromosome 1 that contained 126 genes.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Symptomatic epilepsy and skull dysplasia | 6 | 1 | rs2153337 | rs2225251 | 6.6 | 126"],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:22279524:2","pmid":"22279524","section":"population_genetics","statement":"Puffenberger et al. (2012) found 5 carriers of the SNIP1 c.1097A>G variant among 203 Old Order Amish controls (2.5%).","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["An unlabeled probe melting analysis for this variant identified 5 carriers among 203 Old Order Amish controls (2.5%) (Table 4)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:22279524:3","pmid":"22279524","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Puffenberger et al. (2012) reported that the mutated SNIP1 residue, Glu366, is highly conserved and lies in the C terminus, where SNIP1 interacts with c-Myc, SMAD1 and SMAD2. PolyPhen-2 predicted the p.Glu366Gly substitution to be probably damaging.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["SNIP1 Glu366 is highly conserved and the p.Glu366Gly mutation is in the C-terminus where SNIP1 interacts with c-Myc [48], Smad1 and Smad2 [46], [49].","PolyPhen-2 predicts the p.Glu366Gly substitution to be “probably damaging” (score = 0.99)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:22279524:4","pmid":"22279524","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Puffenberger et al. (2012) expressed mouse Snip1 in mIMCD3 cells. Wild-type Snip1 localized to the nucleus in a punctate pattern, whereas the mutant corresponding to human p.Glu366Gly (mouse p.Glu353Gly) looked more aggregated and was unstable, with 84.9% lower band density than wild type. Transcript levels of the mutant were comparable to wild type.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["However, Snip1 p.Glu353Gly (corresponding to human p.Glu366Gly) had a more aggregated appearance (Figure 3, E) and Western blotting proved it unstable (Figure 3, F); its band density was 84.9±9.6% SD lower than wild-type (n = 4 independent transfections each).","Reverse transcriptase-PCR suggests that the Snip1 c.1058A>G (corresponding to human SNIP1 c.1097A>G) transcript is expressed at levels comparable to wild-type (Figure 3, F)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:22279524:5","pmid":"22279524","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Puffenberger et al. (2012) proposed that reduced SNIP1 abundance likely lowers c-Myc activity and increases TGF-beta and NF-kappa-B signaling. They noted that such changes have been linked to abnormal development of the brain, skull and limbs and to epileptogenesis in mice.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Decreased abundance of SNIP1 likely results in decreased c-Myc activity and increased TGF-β and NF-κB signaling.","Inappropriate increases in TGF-β/Smad2 signaling and in NF-κB signaling in the mouse brain have been shown to independently initiate epileptogenesis [55], [56]."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]}],"dropped":{"records":[],"notes":[]},"gene_decisions":{"candidates":["SNIP1"],"confirmed":["SNIP1"],"dropped":[],"not_confirmed":[]},"candidate_inheritance":["AR"]}